## Dependency graph and execution modes

~~~text
DC #333  Asset generator and pack/catalogue (ordinary leaf)
   |
   +--> DC #334  IndexedDB storage/cache (speculative against A V1)
          |
          +--> DC #335  Live get_favicon integration
                 |       (speculative against B V1)
                 v
              DC #336  Hosted/real-browser acceptance (test preparation
                 |     may overlap; authoritative completion only after
                 |     exact real integration of #333/#334/#335)
                 v
              DC #332  Parent convergence
~~~

Actual graph also contains DC #333 -> DC #335, DC #333 -> DC #336 and
DC #334 -> DC #336 through their transitive prerequisites.  Nothing is
removed by speculative execution.

- #333 ordinary work can be certified independently for its own asset
  generator and A V1 conformance; do not claim it proves runtime integration.
- #334 may complete an isolated implementation and contract tests
  *speculatively* against A V1 fixtures.  Canonical issue stays BLOCKED
  by DC #333 until exact real-pack integration and tests pass.
- #335 may complete an isolated implementation and contract tests
  *speculatively* against a B V1 session stub.  Canonical issue stays
  BLOCKED by DC #333 and DC #334 until exact integrated runtime tests pass.
- #336 may prepare fixtures, test plans and nonintegrated harnesses now.
  It cannot certify the feature until the three implementations converge.
- Never mark a blocked ticket DONE, close it, or advance dependents because
  a synthetic fixture, mock, stub or draft PR passed.

## Work ownership / collision avoidance

All workers must inspect current main and existing branches/PRs first,
recover any existing ownership, and avoid duplicate claims or branches.
Use one worker per issue with separate issue-owned branch/commits/PR.
Do not edit another worker's owned source, assets, tests or contract file.

- #333 owns deterministic generator, source favicon provenance,
  binary asset packs/catalogue, fixtures and pack-format tests.
- #334 owns binary transport, IndexedDB schema, concurrency locks,
  Blob retrieval, expiration and cache-provider tests.
- #335 owns `get_favicon`, favicon lifecycle presentation,
  pulse timer integration, URL mapping and consumer regression tests.
- #336 owns end-to-end integration matrix, browser/CPU measurement
  scripts, exact-source certification and user acceptance handoff.
- #332 owns this PMD, the immutable interface and cross-ticket coordination.
  No worker edits `docs/FAVICON_CACHE_INTERFACES_V1.md` in place.

Where shared configuration is unavoidable, prepare a small isolated diff
or patch/commit for later integration and explicitly coordinate ownership.
Do not cherry-pick or combine changing candidate PRs without certifying
exactly which commits and source they represent.  Avoid implementation
changes in #336 except integration/test-correction changes within scope.

## Non-negotiable design points

- Catalogue `version` is *minimum* compatible major.minor.patch boundary;
  issue suffixes are **ignored entirely**, never ordered.
- `update_every_ms` controls scheduling, not an image key.
- All PNG bytes originate from deterministic, separately stored assets.
  A single immutable, hash-checked binary pack per dataset is fetched and
  stored as Blob records.  No embedded Base64, no full-set tab preload.
- Lookup uses base types 0 yellow, 1 green, 2 red, 3 white;
  pie indexes 0–6; and 21 indexed full-composite pulse frames.  The
  `get_favicon` caller passes time offset, not a precomputed frame index.
- Existing site favicon candidates and all previously accepted Agent
  processing/completion/error/reload/focus acknowledgement semantics survive.
- `last_used` is throttled to 10-minute writes; true stale data is
  removed after >10 minutes only under safe shared/exclusive locking.
- Missing or unsafe data fails closed to original favicon with bounded
  diagnostics.  Do not add duplicate Agent lifecycle detectors.
- Do not merge any PR without explicit user authorization.

## Execution, tests and publication

Start by recording actual wall-clock start time and owning
repository/branch/commit.  Apply AUTONOMOUS_EXECUTION_WINDOW_RULE for
25-minute maximum *per execution window*.  Work continuously until a
genuine stop condition or the measured window expires.  At periodic
updates include elapsed wall-clock time, completed work, currently
running checks and remaining gates; updates are not permission to stop.
Follow current rule's exact handoff format for incomplete windows.

Before changes, add RED regression tests for missing functionality.
Validate GREEN, unchanged existing tests, source syntax, doc conventions,
source files below 500 lines, package/manifest byte identity, and exact
hosted CI.  Use the repository's ordinary RWF-authorized CI request
rather than new temporary workflows or workflow source-writing hacks.
Never claim an upstream conformance PASS from only a mock fixture.
The authoritative generated userscript bytes must be represented in
the repository and tied to the tested commit.  Do not bypass CI.

Keep `DESIGN.md` updated when an actually implemented architectural
change becomes true.  Do not claim planned features already landed.
Publish precise issue/PR evidence with changed files, tested commit,
CI/job links, RED/GREEN results, remaining dependency blockers and
next action.  Where manual browser tests are necessary, provide a
**direct** tested userscript artifact link plus explicit commands and
expected results.  Do not ask users to locate an artifact by searching.

## Required status labels and handoffs

Report clearly one of: ordinary implementation certified,
speculative implementation + contract tests complete (still BLOCKED),
integration/certification incomplete, or truly DONE.  Do not call
speculative completion DONE.  Include source revision and contract
SHA on every handoff.  If unable to continue, report precise
`BLOCKED by DC #...` dependencies and evidence rather than inventing
a blocker.  Never infer user authorization to merge.

